Build all examples using "make" or "make all"

To build examples individually, use "make" followed by the filename (omitting the
file extension). For example, to build the hello world program, use "make hello"

To remove all the executables, run "make clean"

Requirments to build and run the examples:
  * Have the SOS installation "bin" directory set in the environment PATH
  * Have a compatible process launcher (e.g. Hydra version 3.2) set in the
    environment PATH
    * By invoking the run script, "oshrun", it will pick up the chosen process
      launcher.
  * An example of setting the environment PATH on bash:
    $ export PATH=<path-to-SOS-install>/bin:<path-to-hydra-install>/bin:$PATH

The hello world example can be run with 4 processes, as below: 
  oshrun -n 4 ./hello

For more detailed information visit the Getting Started Guide:
  https://github.com/Sandia-OpenSHMEM/SOS/wiki/Getting-Started-Guide

The Licensing information can be found in the "LICENSE" file

ofi_epoch_quiet.c checks remote completion and source reuse across multiple
destinations, puts, signals, non-fetching atomics, repeated quiet epochs, and
default/created contexts.
The regression alternates isolated puts, batches, isolated scalar puts, and
atomic-only epochs to check hybrid completion and cumulative counter accounting.
Isolated large NBI and strided puts cover completion after transport fragmentation
and element/block decomposition; the public API does not expose which completion
branch ran.  Run it with
at least two PEs using the OFI transport.  A useful configuration matrix includes
BOUNCE_SIZE=0 and an enabled bounce buffer, single-endpoint and separate-endpoint
providers, both thread-completion settings, and messages larger than the
provider's maximum message size (to exercise fragmentation).  The burst of
512 scalar puts per destination also exercises progress under queue pressure;
providers with small transmit queues make this check more demanding.  The test uses
shmem_sync_all after quiet so a barrier's implicit quiet cannot hide missing
completions.  Build with "make ofi_epoch_quiet" and run with, for example,
"oshrun -n 4 ./ofi_epoch_quiet".
